## 7. Build Guidance [Designed — assumes Next.js/React/Tailwind per your existing stack; confirm per Q4]

**Core technique: mask-windowed reveal, not per-region remounting.** Build the HUD grid as ONE continuous layer sized to the full surface (same dimensions as the background plate), always mounted and always animating its flicker/scan-pulse internally. Visibility is controlled entirely by a CSS `mask-image`/`-webkit-mask-image` radial-gradient (or an SVG `<mask>` with a `<circle>`) whose center tracks the cursor. This avoids mounting/unmounting grid cells as the cursor moves, which is the difference between smooth and janky.

**Layer stack (z-index, back to front):**
1. Background plate — static image or muted looping video of Earth/nebula. If idle ambient drift is confirmed (Q5), it lives here, independent of hover state.
2. HUD layer (full-surface, always mounted) — SVG grid: lines, nodes, labels, scan-pulse, exactly as extracted in sections 3-4. This layer's own animations (flicker, scan-pulse) run continuously regardless of mask position — the mask decides what's *visible*, not what's *animating*. Simpler to reason about, and nothing pops when the mask arrives at a new region because the content underneath was already live.
3. Mask controller — a single absolutely-positioned div (or the `mask` attribute on layer 2 directly) whose `--mx`/`--my` CSS custom properties are updated on `pointermove`, driving a `radial-gradient(circle at var(--mx) var(--my), white 0%, white 60%, transparent 100%)` mask. Position updates via `requestAnimationFrame` with the lerp described in section 5, not directly on the event.

**Component shape (React):**
```
<CosmicHoverSurface>
  <BackgroundPlate />              // img/video, idle-only motion if any
  <HudLayer maskPos={lerpedPos}/>  // full-surface SVG, always animating internally,
                                    // clipped via CSS mask driven by maskPos
    // internally: <GridLines/> <Nodes/> <TelemetryLabels/> <ScanPulse/>
</CosmicHoverSurface>
```

**Pointer handling:**
- `onPointerMove` on the surface writes raw x/y into a ref (not state — avoid re-render per mousemove).
- A single `requestAnimationFrame` loop reads the raw target, lerps the displayed mask position toward it, writes the result to a CSS custom property via direct DOM (`el.style.setProperty`). This is the only place layout-adjacent work happens, and it's not React state, so it doesn't re-render the tree 60x/sec.
- `onPointerEnter`/`onPointerLeave` on the surface toggle the enter/exit fade (opacity/scale on the mask container), independent of the position loop.

**Performance rules (non-negotiable for a premium feel):**
- Mask position updates: `transform`/CSS-custom-property + `requestAnimationFrame`, never React state per frame.
- Label flicker: throttled interval (~100-150ms), direct `textContent` write via ref, not state.
- Scan-pulse: `stroke-dashoffset`/opacity only.
- Respect `prefers-reduced-motion`: disable lerp-follow (snap instead), disable flicker/scan-pulse, disable idle drift — show a static grid inside a static, larger reveal region on focus/hover instead.
- Keep grid line count modest (source uses ~6-10 lines on screen at once) — since only a portion is visible through the mask at any time, don't over-populate the full layer either; density should look right at the mask's scale, not at the full-surface scale.

